[h1]Heroes Evolve[/h1]

In vanilla, AI lords keep the same equipment and skills from 18 until they die.
This mod makes them develop: they gain skills over the years, buy their own gear
in town markets, and take it off each other after a battle.

Every system can be switched off on its own. Nothing is written to your save, and
the mod is safe to add to a campaign in progress or remove from one.

[h2]Starting Kit[/h2]
[list]
[*]Some lords come of age in civilian clothes, with one weapon, or with most
skills at zero, and stay that way for life. (Known TW bug)
[*]They get a plain kit, matched to culture, battlefield role and age. Anything
better they buy themselves.
[*]Only for lords who never had a loadout. A man robbed of his is handled under
Robbery.
[/list]

[h2]Not every lord fights like his culture[/h2]
[list]
[*]Occasionally a lord comes of age armed for what he is good at instead of what
his culture teaches: a Vlandian crossbowman on foot, a Khuzait archer who never
took to the horse, a Sturgian javelin rider.
[*]Always within what that culture's troops actually field, read from the troop
trees, so it works with modded cultures. Never a Vlandian archer, a Battanian
horse archer or a mounted Nord.
[/list]

[h2]Skills grow over time[/h2]
[list]
[*]Lords, companions and wanderers gain skills in the weapons they carry and the
trades they have focus points in.
[*]Each hero has a hidden talent in three areas: combat, management, seafaring.
Set at creation, fixed for life, independent of each other. It decides how fast
he learns and where he stops.
[*]Lords TaleWorlds wrote by hand follow their own skill profile: below it while
young, level with it at about 55, 5% above it at 60.
[*]About 1 hero in 200 ends above the strongest lord in the base game. It can be
a companion you hired.
[*]Reaches lords sitting in castles and wanderers waiting in taverns, so a
companion hired late is already developed.
[*]A new campaign starts every lord at skills matching his age, and so does any
lord who appears later. Campaigns in progress are left alone.
[/list]

[h2]Lords buy their own gear[/h2]
[list]
[*]In a town, a lord buys better equipment with his clan's money.
[*]Capped by his skills: a weak lord cannot buy his way into top-tier armour.
[*]Only upgrades what he already carries. Never a weapon type he did not have,
never an empty slot, never a mount for a man who fought on foot, camels only if
his culture rides them.
[*]Equip a companion, give him a party, and he improves that loadout instead of
replacing it.
[*]Worst piece of armour replaced first.
[*]His own culture's gear counts 25% better to him. Can be locked to his own
culture only.
[*]Richer clans equip their lords better. A reserve is held back so shopping
never leaves a clan unable to pay its troops.
[/list]

[h2]Caravan masters buy their own armour[/h2]
[list]
[*]Takes a commission on what his caravan earns and buys gear with that, not
with your treasury.
[*]Starts with nothing and never spends more than he has earned.
[*]You set the commission. Higher arms him better and pays you less. He takes
nothing while he has nothing left to improve.
[*]Bandits who beat his caravan capture and strip him.
[/list]

[h2]Robbery[/h2]
[list]
[*]Talk to a lord you hold prisoner and you can take his weapons and armour,
unique gear included.
[*]AI lords rob their prisoners too. That gear stays in the world: they wear it
or sell it, and you can win it back by beating them.
[*]Nobody is left naked. A robbed man is dressed again in the same instant, in
the cheapest gear of the same kinds he was carrying, mount included.
[*]Loadouts survive capture. Equip a companion as a crossbowman, lose him, and
he comes back with a crossbow. A man who fought on foot stays on foot. Only
slots that held something get something.
[*]Rags are not worth stealing, so a man already at the bottom is left alone.
[*]You are told when anyone in your clan or kingdom is robbed, directly under
the game's own capture notice.
[*]Costs you relation with the man you rob and a hit to your honour. A
dishonourable prisoner costs half, a friend costs full.
[*]To do it: hold him prisoner in your party or in a settlement your clan owns,
talk to him, choose [i]"Hand over your arms and armour."[/i]
[/list]

An AI captor's chance comes from his character. Each trait on its own, with the
other three neutral, at default settings:

[code]
                 -2      -1       0      +1      +2
Honour         15.8%   10.3%    6.2%    3.4%    1.6%
Generosity     12.9%    9.2%    6.2%    4.0%    2.4%
Mercy          12.9%    9.2%    6.2%    4.0%    2.4%
Calculating     8.1%    7.1%    6.2%    5.4%    4.7%
[/code]

They stack: all four at -1 is 21.1%, all four neutral 6.2%, all four at +1 is
0.8%. All +2 never robs, all -2 robs half the time. In practice traits sit
between -1 and +1.

Then adjusted: Roguery up to +50% at 300. Relation +100 = never, -100 = +50%.
Clansmen x0.25. Close family never, unless relation is under -50. Bandits ignore
all of it and sit at a flat 50%.

One slider, [b]How often lords rob[/b], multiplies every figure above. It runs
0 to 2 and ships at 1.00.

[code]
0.00   no AI lord robs -- you can still rob in conversation
1.00   a normal campaign: the numbers above, as printed
2.00   every figure doubled; robbery then outweighs skill, clan wealth
       and the market as the reason a lord wears what he does
[/code]

[h2]Keeping other mods' gear out[/h2]
[list]
[*]Gear packs add hundreds of high-tier items, and one pack can end up dressing
the whole map.
[*]MCM screen [i]Heroes Evolve - Excluded gear[/i] lists every gear mod you have
installed, with a tick box each. Ticked, AI lords never buy or receive its
items. The mod itself is untouched: you and your troops keep using it.
[*]Individual item ids can be excluded on the same screen.
[/list]

[h2]Requirements[/h2]
[list]
[*]Mod Configuration Menu (MCM) v5, for the in-game options screen.
[/list]

[h2]Configuration[/h2]
[list]
[*]Every system can be switched off on its own.
[*]Changes apply immediately: no restart, no new campaign.
[*]Tune how freely lords spend, whose culture they favour, how often they rob,
the gear tier ceiling, what a caravan pays its master, and which items or mods
are excluded.
[*]Choose whether your own clan is managed by the mod or left entirely to you.
[*]A console command writes a survey of every lord in your campaign to a log.
[/list]

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]
[list]
[*]Writes nothing into your save.
[*]Works with mods that add items or cultures.
[*]Built and tested with War Sails. Does not require it.
[/list]

[nexus-only]
[h2]Installation[/h2]

[list=1]
[*]Drop the [i]HeroesEvolve[/i] folder into
[i]Mount & Blade II Bannerlord/Modules/[/i].
[*]Tick Heroes Evolve in the launcher's module list.
[*]Load order sorts itself: the module declares what it needs.
[/list]

[/nexus-only]
[h2]Languages[/h2]

English, Spanish, French, German, Russian, Turkish.
